namespace OpenTPW;

/// <summary>
/// Register of the original-save import mappings that are not traced (docs/reverse/SAVE-plan.md §8.2,
/// docs/SAVE-CONTAINER.md, "Import into OpenTPW"). Each entry has a matching <c>// [APPROX:SAVE-NNN]</c>
/// comment at its code site. Values the import drops are also reported in <see cref="OriginalEconomyImport.Evidence"/>.
/// </summary>
public static class SaveApproximations
{
	public static readonly IReadOnlyList<(string Id, string Assumption, string EvidenceNeeded)> All = new[]
	{
		("SAVE-002", "a saved guest in a queue or boarding (states 11–14), leaving a ride (15), using an attraction (16) or held by a guard (17) is not imported, nor is a park guest off the paths, a guard escorting a guest is imported idle and one chasing a guest the import does not restore gives the chase up at his first update; an imported guest in an activity (state 8) decides again at once, one at the booth (4) that was not yet weighed waits OpenTPW's booth time (ARRIVAL-006), a turn stamp before the save's turn counts from the import, and the admission flag (mBeenAdmitted, set by OnAdmittance 0x5012B0 and cleared by the dismissal 0x5012F0, which also clears the queue links), the 32-slot event ring (AddEvent 0x50C100) and the queue links (mQNext, mQPrev) are not imported", "an OpenTPW queue and rider restore for original guests"),
		("SAVE-004", "a candidate saved with its on-pointer byte set (+0xB, picked up for placement) is imported as an ordinary pool candidate: the placement object in the player's hand is not saved or recreated, where the original keeps the flag until the carry tool ends (0x5083B0 then removes the candidate); the pool's sub-types are imported as the candidates' sprite variants (0x507600, 0x541F70, kept by the member hired from the candidate), and the people-in-category counts and stop-producing flags need no import: the census 0x508000 recomputes them from the park's staff before every read (the refill 0x5084F0, its top-up 0x508170 and the pick-up 0x507BF0), as UpdatePool counts the hired staff; the opening-pool flag only gates the opening pool 0x5087D0, which OpenTPW draws for a new park only", "what the hire list and the refill do with a flagged candidate while no placement exists (none in the corpus: every saved on-pointer byte is 0)"),
		("SAVE-006", "a staff claim no imported job holds is not imported (an imported job's claim is taken again with its saved turn, 0x004E01F0, and a claim lapses 100 turns after its turn unless its claimant still targets the object, 0x004E0220, ECON-022); a player's own save's park gate gives the park its name (its two sign lines) and an idle attraction saved with its open word clear stays closed, the shipped Easymode seed keeps the gate's default lines in the player's language (they are the same text); the other attractions' sign lines are their OBJECT_NAMES row, and the raw links (mesh instance, script and track handles, flags, footprint, entrance and exit cells, upgrade parent, balloon sprite) are rebuilt by the placement and the queue (cells, ends, called guest) from the laid queue cells", "a save whose attraction is claimed by a staff member with no imported job on it (none in the corpus: no corpus park employs staff)"),
		("SAVE-007", "a lab project of an object the level archives do not define (a modified save) and a saved current project OpenTPW's picker would not offer now (its group closed, its add-on target not researched) are dropped; the saved highest open group of each category is imported as saved (the original's loader reads it back as it is). The lab's table order is the original's object scan (ECON-017) and the 11 corpus labs list exactly OpenTPW's projects, less the base projects of cost 0, in that order; the object controls' researched bytes (the control manager's table 0x4D3AA0 after the world variables, SaveObjectControls) are read and checked: only the lab's init 0x5031A0 (an object without a research cost) and the research step 0x504630 write them, and they agree with OpenTPW's research for every research item of every corpus save", "a save that holds a project of an object absent from the level archives or a current project the picker would not offer (the 11 corpus labs hold neither)"),
		("SAVE-009", "a member saved in state 7 (picked up) with the staff HQ's picked-up id (+0x60) naming him is imported idle (StaffPlacement.Reconcile): the original's only reader of the id is the drop 0x509AF0 from the carry tool's exit 0x46CC40 (which also clears it), and no carry tool exists after a load, so the original keeps such a member in state 7 for good, the pick-up guard 0x505BD0 refusing state 7. The force-strike word and the trained byte need no import: the force strike (+0x64) is written only by the HQ constructor 0x5089C0 (0) and the save loader 0x508BB0, so the month gate 0x508E70 and the grievance 0x509360 see 0 in play as OpenTPW does, and the trained byte (mHaveEverTrained, training +0x14) is only cleared (0x50C620) and serialized (0x50C640), never read", "nothing unread: whether OpenTPW keeps its kinder reconciliation of a member saved in state 7 or reproduces the original's limbo is a design decision for the user"),
		("SAVE-010", "the advisor presentation block (its width is traced, SaveBlockWidths), the first clock word (the game clock the saved scripts' waits count from, needed with the RSSE run state; the second is the advisor clock, applied when the automatic advisor attaches), vanilla time, game-system words, cheat bytes (OpenTPW has no cheat codes), advisor scoring and UI record count (SaveRuntimeBlocks), the Coasters block, whose structure SaveCoasterBlock reads (SAVE-920 to SAVE-923) without importing any state, the weather, park analyser, tag system, mechanic HQ and UI message receiver things, the world state, the arrival vehicle handles and bytes (the vehicle at work belongs to its script's run, which restarts, ARRIVAL-001) and the calendar session start, and the advisor controller's time signature, sound, three raw words, mRequestedLastTutorial and mMessagePlaying are not imported (its current record is imported as the last tutorial advice when an automatic advisor attaches), nor are its pending advice and history when no automatic advisor is attached; the Sound block holds the sound system's own state (its flag, 84 bytes of handles and volumes at 0x803AA0, and QMixer's chunk, loader 0x51C400), which OpenTPW's audio does not share ([EXT:original-engine-state-dumps]); the camera is imported (zoom, yaw and the required point of interest, loader 0x42CEC0, a level starts its camera there) without the loader's yaw correction by the word 0x78D912; of the cell map the path cells, occupancy, MAP attributes, each path cell's texture and turn and the litter (type, collector and claim turn, with the handyman jobs on it) are read, and a saved path tile is drawn only while the cell's path neighbours equal the saved ones and its turn is 0/90/180/270 (otherwise the tile is chosen from the neighbours)", "the vehicle's script run for the world state and the arrival words (the saved RSSE variables and program counters and the game clock they are timed on), the camera loader's yaw correction word 0x78D912, and the original's reconstruction of the weather, park analyser, tag system, mechanic HQ and message receiver things (no restore of their own was found)"),
		("SAVE-011", "a Coasters block with a coaster is located by its delimiter's bytes (every other block ends where its reader stops, SaveBlockWidths, the World included since the loader 0x5179C0 is traced, then its tag must follow, like tp.exe's loader 0x415270; a Theme Park World payload's World that does not read as the original's, a synthetic payload, falls back to its delimiter too; the Theme Park Inc profile has no such fallback and reads its World exactly): a payload that splits in more than one way there, or holds the COAS delimiter's bytes more than MaximumDelimiterOccurrences times, is rejected, while the original loader runs each block's own reader and then compares the next 4 bytes with the block's tag (the tags at 0x415371 and its successors), never searching, and loads such payloads", "the coaster type's train slot and car counts (type +0x2E8 and +0x2F4) for a layout that knows the park's coaster types"),
		("SAVE-012", "Restart Park reloads OpenTPW's own snapshot (opentpw-<theme>-restart.json, the park-resume format), not the original's <save>\\restart.INTS: the original writes that file in state 15 of the park state machine 0x54E360 through 0x550B30 (skipped while the GameType word 0xFB3B7C is 1, the online visit) and reloads it for a restart request (code 1 from the confirm callbacks 0x48B5B0 and 0x48B620 through 0x5508B0: state 10 to 13, which only advances, and 14, where 0x5996D0(1) ends the running park and 0x5AC5F0 loads the file through 0x414D40, then back to 10) and takes no new snapshot after that reload; OpenTPW restarts into a park-resume snapshot instead of an original save file (which snapshot is kept is the design choice [EXT:restart-snapshot-per-start]; the camera block that is not restored belongs to SAVE-010)", "whether OpenTPW's park-resume snapshot restores everything the original's restart.INTS load does (the writer 0x5ac780 and loader 0x414D40 are the save format's twins, SAVE-010 lists what the import leaves out)"),
		("SAVE-013", "the Scripts block (SaveScriptsBlock: the sprite-script slot table and each 0x118-byte VM record, tp.exe 0x475730) holds one sprite script per saved guest or staff member (the person's script handle, created by 0x4D4140 from the program table 0x75A418: entry 0x5A is the standing loop that holds frame 0, which the staff drop 0x505EA0 sets as program 3, entry 0x2A the walking one; each park save of the corpus holds one script per saved guest) and one per litter item (0x4D93B0, the programs 0x74F050 to 0x74F168 with the litter type); its records are read exactly and not applied: OpenTPW's guests and staff animate from their state and clock and its litter sprites from the cell (the programs are threaded code, handler pointers with inline operands: 0x476940 sets a frame, 0x476020 jumps), so the saved program counters, wake ticks (+0x7C) and periods have no counterpart; the Particles block's pool is applied to the level's particle system (SaveParticleState maps it partially: it drops the emitter hold, group, screen-space, ring and immortal words, the bounds, the effector group, mode and strength, the pool capacity, density and low-detail flag and the saved template tables, so modified templates 101 to 104 restore as the file's; the corpus loses nothing), and the post-load pass 0x5200B0 hides the screen-space emitters (the saved Button sparkles), leaving Jungle's WaterFall; the effector slot stores no template id, which SaveParticleState recovers from template bytes the tick does not write by direct displacement (writes through a base register were not checked; no corpus save has a live effector)", "whether the saved animation phase of a guest or litter sprite (frame, wake tick, period) shows anything OpenTPW's state-derived frames lack, and a save with a live effector to confirm the template match"),
		("SAVE-920", "no runtime coaster is rebuilt from a decoded Coasters block (the body between the stored EMAK and SAOC delimiters): the reader decodes the headers, sections and trains of every saved coaster once the coaster type's train and car counts are known (the park import takes them from the level's coaster .sam), but ParkObjects.ImportOriginal refuses a park whose block lists a coaster, because the original's loader (tp.exe 0x437B60) rebuilds each coaster's controller, track nodes, links, trains and cars from it and OpenTPW's coaster runtime has no such restore from the original's words; a park without coaster imports as before", "a Theme Park World save with a built coaster to check the decoded block on real bytes, and the restore of a CoasterRide's track and trains from SaveCoaster, SaveCoasterSection and SaveCoasterTrain"),
		("SAVE-921", "the coaster header's flag word is kept raw: the saver (tp.exe 0x437300) writes controller +0x3C bits 0 and 1 as wire 0x1 and 0x2 (the track is complete, the circuit is cut open: SaveCoaster.TrackComplete and CircuitOpen) and the track flags (+0) 0x20 to 0x800 as wire 0x4 to 0x100 (0x43743C..0x4374D0), and the loader maps them back (0x4380D4..0x438335); the section flag word is decoded (SaveCoasterSectionFlags: wire 0x1/0x2/0x4 and the cut, link and intersection marks are restored, wire 0x8/0x10/0x20 are the loop and zero-bank flags the node constructor derives again), but the meaning of the track flags 0x20, 0x80, 0x100, 0x200 and 0x400 is not traced (0x40 is the outside mode and 0x800 is cleared when the trains are removed, COAST-plan §22)", "what sets and reads the controller's track flags 0x20, 0x80, 0x100, 0x200 and 0x400 (controller +0)"),
		("SAVE-922", "the coaster header words +12, +20, +28, +32, +244 and +240 and the block's three globals are saved but only partly understood, they are reported raw. Traced in tp.exe's loader 0x437B60: the controller word +16 is the station's mesh instance id (SaveCoaster.StationMeshInstanceId) matched against the live coasters' +0x10 to bind the block to an existing coaster (0x437CBF; none matching dereferences null, 0x437CD5), +8 is the controller state word (the low nibble is the CoasterControllerState), the three globals and the words +20 and +28 go to the coaster slot allocator 0x468760 (state 0x790B04, 0x790B00, 0x791CE0), whose slot is stored in the controller's +0x14, and +240 is restored to the controller's +0xF0 (0x438373), which 0x43BA90 copies to the room capacity +0xB8 (0x43BBD1); the section words are decoded (the mesh instance +112, bank +216, incline +220, yaw +224, height parameter +232 and loop rotation +248, setters 0x444150, 0x4441D0 and 0x444270) and the auxiliary ordinals are the link ordinals of the node with ordinal 2", "the readers of the controller words +12, +32 and +244 and what the slot allocator 0x468760 does with +20 and +28 and the three globals"),
		("SAVE-923", "the train words +0x38, +0x64 and +0x68, the car's flag word and the car byte +0x44 are kept raw, no original coaster chunk is written. The layout is decoded in tp.exe (saver 0x437300, loader 0x437B60) and read by SaveCoasterTrain: per train slot a 27-byte record (train flags +0, the car count +0xC, +0x74 the reference car's position, +0x78 the speed, +0x10 the riders; 0x43783B), then per car an 11-byte record (car +0, counts A and B from car +0x30/+0x34, +0x44, the car's mesh instance +0xC; 0x4378A5) and A then B u32 entries from the car's lists +0x28/+0x2C (the riders on the lap and the guests seated at the station); nothing in the train step 0x43CE20, the layout 0x43CBDA or the station step 0x43B8B0 reads the raw words", "the writers and readers of train +0x38, +0x64 and +0x68 and of car +0 and +0x44"),
		("SAVE-940", "the Theme Park Inc attraction thing (ThemeParkIncAttraction, serializer 0x5dc430) reads the three unnamed 4-byte words after mQueueSizeInCells as the gauges of the Theme Park World serializer (Gauge48, LifeGauge, StateOfRepair), and keeps its 19 history rings (one inline, eleven through 0x5ea720 and six through 0x5eac40, thirteen of 30 entries and six of 144) in serializer order without their meanings: the loader's field log names none of them, the costs, takings and customer counts the Theme Park World attraction stores as words are not among its fields, and the prebuilt's rings are all empty", "a Theme Park Inc save whose attractions have run, or the names of the history fields"),
		("SAVE-941", "the 36-byte Theme Park Inc object control (control manager 0x5cf9b0) is read as the Theme Park World 32-byte record (id, cost, +8, +0xC, researched at +0x10, then +0x14, +0x18, +0x1C) plus a word at +0x20: the loader writes each record as one 0x24-byte block, so the new field's place follows the control struct's layout, which the save cannot show", "the control struct's field list from its constructor or the research code"),
		("SAVE-942", "the Theme Park Inc calendar's field order (two 8-byte stamps, the year at last update, the zero-based month, the day, the rate) is read from the prebuilt's bytes (2000, 0, 4, 15000), not from the serializer's field log, and the 18 arrival bytes stay raw", "the macro AI's calendar and arrival serializers' field lists"),
		("SAVE-943", "the Theme Park Inc World's special rides (loader 0x6244b0, a u32 count and records of a u16 and 20 slots of 10 bytes) are sized by the saved count word, bounded at 1024: the loader loops by a live global rather than the saved count, so a save whose count differs from that global (the prebuilt's is 4) reads differently there; the records stay raw", "the global's value and writer at the loader's loop, and a save with a different count"),
		("SAVE-944", "the Theme Park Inc thing models 4 to 9 (staff and the staff HQ), 11 (advisor controller), 13 (park analyser), 14 (research lab), 15 (weather), 19 (challenge system) and 20 (gardener) and the guests are kept raw. The staff HQ, advisor controller, park analyser, research lab, weather and challenge system take the prebuilt's body widths (411, 233, 76,617, 8,414, 104 and 1,287 bytes, checked by the thing header or ENOD that must follow), not widths read from their serializers; guests, staff and gardeners, absent from the prebuilt, have no width and end at the first following thing header that reads, so a complete header lookalike inside such a body is not detected (a raw body can always absorb the thing after it, so a uniqueness check on the segmentation is not possible)", "the field lists of those models' serializers and a Theme Park Inc save with guests, staff or a gardener"),
